# Nothing may be indented under a block written on its header's line.
#
# The header's line held the whole block, so an indented line after it
# opens a block belonging to nothing: the `if` is finished, and the
# statement below is neither its body nor a statement of the enclosing
# block, which is at a shallower level. Reading it as either would be a
# guess, so it is refused where it is written.
#
# This program is expected to be REFUSED. Its `.ast.out` holds the error
# message, not a program's output.

x = 3

if x > 2: print('big')
    print('indented under nothing')
